<section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-24">
  <header class="max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">
      Filter/Search
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl">
      Privacy Policy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-muted">
      Filter/Search is being built around a simple principle: people should
      have more control over their experience of the web, including how
      information about their browsing is handled.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      Last updated: October 18, 2026
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-10 leading-7 text-muted">

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">1. Overview</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Filter/Search is a browser extension and web project focused on
        user-controlled browsing, content filtering, and greater transparency
        about online experiences.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        This policy explains what information Filter/Search handles, why that
        information may be needed, how it is stored or shared, and what choices
        users have. The final policy must reflect the behavior of the published
        extension and any services it actually uses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">2. Information the extension accesses</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Depending on the permissions and features enabled, a browser extension
        may access information about the pages on which it operates. This can
        include page text, page structure, URLs, or other information necessary
        to identify content and apply user-configured rules.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Filter/Search should access only the information reasonably necessary
        for its disclosed functionality. Access to a page does not, by itself,
        mean that its contents are transmitted to a remote server.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong class="text-ink">Before publication:</strong> Specify the actual
        Chrome permissions, supported websites, information read from pages,
        and whether any browsing activity or page content is collected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">3. How information is used</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Information accessed by the extension may be used to provide its
        filtering and browsing features, apply user preferences, identify
        supported page elements, and help users understand or customize their
        browsing experience.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Information should not be used for unrelated purposes without
        appropriate disclosure and, where required, user consent.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong class="text-ink">Before publication:</strong> Confirm which of
        these activities the current release actually performs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">4. Storage and retention</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Browser extensions may store settings and preferences locally in the
        browser or use remote storage when a feature requires it. The storage
        method determines where information resides and how long it remains.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong class="text-ink">Before publication:</strong> Identify the
        information Filter/Search stores, whether it uses Chrome storage,
        local storage, or a backend, how long information is retained, and
        how users can remove it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">5. Sharing and third parties</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Any information transmitted outside the user's browser should be
        disclosed, including the purpose of the transmission and the parties
        receiving the information.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        This includes applicable analytics, error-reporting tools, external
        APIs, hosting services, and other third-party integrations. Their
        privacy practices may be governed by their own policies.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong class="text-ink">Before publication:</strong> Confirm whether
        any user information is sent to third parties, and identify the
        relevant services. Do not claim that no information is shared unless
        the implementation supports that claim.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">6. Website and external links</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        The Filter/Search website may link to external websites, including its
        source-code repository. External websites operate independently and
        have their own privacy practices.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong class="text-ink">Before publication:</strong> Disclose any
        cookies, analytics, tracking technologies, or other data collection
        used by the website or its hosting services, if applicable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">7. Security</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Reasonable safeguards should be used to protect information handled
        by the project. No method of storage or transmission can be guaranteed
        to be completely secure.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Describe any safeguards that are actually implemented, rather than
        implying protections that have not been verified.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">8. Your choices</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Users should be able to review the browser permissions they grant,
        change available extension settings, and disable or uninstall the
        extension through Chrome.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Uninstalling an extension does not necessarily delete information
        previously transmitted to a remote service. Any remote data deletion
        process should be explained here if applicable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">9. Children's privacy</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        The project should describe whether it is directed toward children
        and how it handles information relating to children where applicable.
        Add a policy consistent with the project's intended audience and
        the laws that apply to its operation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">10. Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        This policy may be updated as Filter/Search develops. The date at the
        top of this page should be revised when material changes are made.
        Changes should accurately describe new permissions, features, data
        handling, or third-party services.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink">11. Contact</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        For questions about this policy or the project's privacy practices,
        contact the project maintainer through the
        <a
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 hover:opacity-80"
          href="https://github.com/PrimeTimeTran/filtersearch"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Filter/Search GitHub repository</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        If a dedicated contact address or issue-reporting process is established,
        include it here.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
</section>
